= Wave 10 compiled context =

'''State: Phase 2 — Context compilation + writing.'''

Wave 10 extends prior probability, causality, agent, systems, network, identity, and security concepts into public decision/institution foundations.

== Dependency order ==

<pre>
Decision theory → Utility (decision theory)
Game theory → Coordination problem / Collective action / Mechanism design
Mechanism design → Incentive / Principal–agent problem
Collective action → Public good / Commons / Governance
Governance → Reputation system → Sybil attack
</pre>

== Normalized definitions ==

=== Decision theory ===
Decision theory studies how choices can be represented, evaluated, or prescribed when an agent faces alternatives, consequences, and often uncertainty.

=== Utility (decision theory) ===
Utility is a numerical representation of preference or value used in decision theory, game theory, and related formal models.

=== Game theory ===
Game theory is the formal study of strategic interaction in which each participant's outcome can depend on the choices of multiple participants.

=== Mechanism design ===
Mechanism design studies how rules or institutions can be constructed so that strategic participants produce desired outcomes under stated informational and incentive constraints.

=== Incentive ===
An incentive is a feature of an environment, rule, or consequence that changes the relative attractiveness of actions to an agent.

=== Coordination problem ===
A coordination problem is a strategic situation in which participants can benefit from matching, aligning, or otherwise making mutually compatible choices but may fail to select the same compatible outcome.

=== Collective action ===
Collective action is coordinated or interdependent action by multiple participants toward outcomes that depend on contributions from more than one participant.

=== Public good ===
A public good is a good whose use is substantially non-rival and from which exclusion is difficult or costly under the relevant technological and institutional conditions.

=== Commons ===
A commons is a shared resource or resource system governed, used, or accessed by a community or multiple participants under some set of formal or informal rules.

=== Principal–agent problem ===
A principal–agent problem arises when one party delegates action to another party whose interests, information, or risk exposure differ in ways that make desired behavior difficult to observe or induce.

=== Governance ===
Governance is the set of processes, rules, institutions, and relationships through which collective decisions are made, implemented, monitored, and revised.

=== Reputation system ===
A reputation system collects, aggregates, and communicates information about participants' past behavior or evaluations to influence future decisions and incentives.

=== Sybil attack ===
A Sybil attack occurs when one adversarial entity creates or controls multiple identities and uses them to gain disproportionate influence in a system that treats those identities as independent participants.

== Required non-equivalences ==

<pre>
decision theory ≠ descriptive psychology by definition
utility ≠ money or happiness by definition
game theory ≠ zero-sum games only
equilibrium ≠ social optimum
mechanism design ≠ generic product design
incentive ≠ reward only
coordination problem ≠ collective-action problem
public good ≠ government-provided good
public good ≠ commons
commons ≠ open access
principal–agent problem ≠ employment only
governance ≠ government
reputation ≠ trust
Sybil attack ≠ pseudonymity
</pre>

